referenceSolutions.python:
def recursiveShelvesPeakTicket(readings, k):
    a=readings
    out=[]
    def visit(left,right):
        if left>=right: return
        out.append(sum(a[left:right]))
        if right-left>1:
            mid=(left+right)//2
            visit(left,mid)
            visit(mid,right)
    visit(0,len(a))
    return max(out,default=0)

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int recursiveShelvesPeakTicket(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {class Walk {void visit(int l,int r){if(l>=r)return;int s=0;for(int i=l;i<r;i++)s+=a[i];out.add(s);if(r-l>1){int m=(l+r)/2;visit(l,m);visit(m,r);}}}new Walk().visit(0,a.length);}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.max(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

Start with the whole readings list as a section. Record the sum of that section. If it has more than one number, split it into a left half and a right half. Use the same steps on the left half first, then on the right half. When a section has an odd length, the left half has one fewer number. A section with one number is not split. Do not record empty sections. The result list contains the sums in the order you record them.

Return the answer

Return the largest number in the result list. If the list is empty, return 0. If all numbers are negative, return the largest negative number; do not replace it with 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n log n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

